To create a custom warning for unsaved changes before leaving a webpage, you can utilize the `window.onbeforeunload` event in JavaScript. However, it's important to note that you cannot fully customize the dialog that appears; you can only set a message that will be displayed alongside a default message.

Here’s a basic implementation:

1. **Track the "dirty" state of your form**: You need to determine if the form has unsaved changes. You can create a function that checks if any form elements have been modified from their default values.

2. **Set up the `onbeforeunload` event**: Assign a function to `window.onbeforeunload` that checks the dirty state and returns a message if there are unsaved changes.

Here’s an example code snippet:

```javascript
function formIsDirty(form) {
    for (var i = 0; i < form.elements.length; i++) {
        var element = form.elements[i];
        var type = element.type;
        if (type === "checkbox" || type === "radio") {
            if (element.checked !== element.defaultChecked) {
                return true;
            }
        } else if (type === "hidden" || type === "password" || type === "text" || type === "textarea") {
            if (element.value !== element.defaultValue) {
                return true;
            }
        } else if (type === "select-one" || type === "select-multiple") {
            for (var j = 0; j < element.options.length; j++) {
                if (element.options[j].selected !== element.options[j].defaultSelected) {
                    return true;
                }
            }
        }
    }
    return false;
}

window.onbeforeunload = function(e) {
    e = e || window.event;
    if (formIsDirty(document.forms["yourFormName"])) {
        var message = "You have unsaved changes!";
        // For IE and Firefox
        if (e) {
            e.returnValue = message;
        }
        // For Safari
        return message;
    }
};
```

Replace `"yourFormName"` with the actual name of your form. This code will prompt the user with a warning if they attempt to leave the page with unsaved changes.

Keep in mind that while you can set a custom message, the browser will still display its own default message along with yours, and you cannot completely replace the dialog box with a custom modal dialog as browsers restrict this behavior for security reasons [How can I override the OnBeforeUnload dialog and replace it with my own?](276660).

To efficiently locate the first character in a string that isn't in a specified collection, you can use a simple method that leverages the `TrimStart` function. Here's a one-liner solution:

